WEBVTT

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:29.960
Dear Mom and Dad, Camp is fun. I wanted to climb the mountain, but everyone says I'm not old enough yet. Can I come back next year and do it? Bye.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:36.040
Nick.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:46.890
Mount St. Helens, a 9,677-foot sleeping volcano nestled in the wild abundance of the Gifford

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:50.680
Pinchot National Forest in southwest Washington.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:28.040
On March 20, 1980, Mount St. Helens began to show tell-tale signs that her slumber was ending.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:48.190
After the last great ice age had retreated northward, the Cascades were already well

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:57.660
established, running from northern California through Oregon, Washington, and into Canada.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.000
But there was one peak amongst them.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:08.130
that was not so well established. Compared to the rest, she was a teenage mountain, and like most teens,

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:16.860
had growing pains. St. Helens is known to have erupted numerous times in the past. In the early

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:22.860
and mid-1800s she erupted again. It's small wonder local Native Americans in a variety

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:28.800
of tribal languages called this peak basically the same thing: Fire Mountain.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.570
The view from the summit of Mount St. Helens was breathtaking.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:37.820
Below lay Spirit Lake.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:43.490
In the distance, an older peak, Mount Rainier.

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:47.390
The lake was a haven for recreational activities and youth camps,

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:50.170
the YMCA's Camp Meehan being the first.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:53.170
Thousands of youngsters flocked to the lake over the years,

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:57.930
learning how to row a boat even when the oarlocks wouldn't stay put.

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.000
Sailing a wind that seemed to

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.000
from the blue-green depths of the lake itself,

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.990
always discovering new friends,

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:07.850
the diamond-encrusted night skies,

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:10.830
and how to cook an egg on a stick.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:16.800
There were national forest campgrounds on the lake, too:

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.720
Bear Cove, Cedar Creek, Donnybrook,

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:23.040
and beyond them stretched the forest,

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.950
a living museum for the curious.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.110
a place as close to heaven as one could get. At trail's end, there were places that were

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:49.510
like living in a postcard world. In the winter of 1979, changes were taking place deep beneath

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:56.550
the mountain's serene exterior. On March 20th, 1980, a moderate earthquake beneath the mountain

00:03:56.550 --> 00:04:00.000
registered 4.1 on the Richter scale and was the first

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:06.300
sign of an eruptive phase. Swarms of quakes followed until March 27th.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:24.990
A concern for public safety prompted the closure of the Gifford Pinchot National Forest. What

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:30.000
scientists had anticipated, the eruption of Mount St. Helens before the end of

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.770
this century had begun. The mountain had a newly opened crater measuring about 250 feet in diameter.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:46.100
The earthquakes continued. Forest Service and cooperating scientists went right to work,

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:52.570
ringing the mountain with an array of gadgets, hoping to document even the

00:04:52.570 --> 00:05:00.000
slightest twinge. The Forest Service ordered immediate evacuation of its own nearby ranger

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.580
such as this one on Pine Creek on the southeastern flank of the mountain. They

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:10.050
also strongly urged others having homes, lodges, or camps near the mountain to do

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:16.060
the same. On the morning of March 29th, a second crater was observed, which had

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:22.200
opened during the night. The new crater measured 75 feet wide and 30 to 60 feet

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:30.000
deep. On March 31st, explosives

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:40.870
eruptions continued and the two craters merged. For the next several days, the

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:45.900
mountain continued to spew ash and steam. Then eruptive activity decreased.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:50.540
However, a bulge noticed earlier on the north-northeast flank of the mountain near

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:55.480
Goat Rocks was found to be moving north-northwest at about five feet a day.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:06:00.000
Scientist David Johnston

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:05.650
expressed the opinions of the U.S. Geological Survey. They would later prove incredibly accurate.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:12.120
In the initial phases, we'd see probably very large eruptions that would extend high into the air.

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:19.410
This would spread ash over large areas in western Washington, maybe possibly in an extreme case,

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:25.580
even farther east. In the near flanks of the volcano, there would be more damage done by

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:30.000
possibly hot avalanches, pyroclastic floats. Farther downstream we could see

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:51.270
Because this is such a symmetrical volcano and because the crater is so high, if there's an explosion, it's probable that the debris, the very hot incandescent ash that would come down, would come down all sides, but right now there's a very great hazard due to the fact that the glacier is breaking up on this side of the volcano, on the north side, and that could produce a very large avalanche hazard.

00:06:51.270 --> 00:07:00.000
In the meantime, the chief of the Forest Service declared part of the land around the mountain a geological area to help protect the unique field.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.660
features.

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:06.060
The Forest Service and the U.S. Geological Survey were taking the mountains seriously,

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:10.720
as did the Spirit Lake Youth Camp operators, who coordinated their efforts, and along with

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:15.020
a donated helicopter and a large refuse bin, began hauling out.

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:20.130
Well, the tourists come to see her smoke, and they all think it's one big joke.

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:23.660
That mountain's just a molehill in disguise.

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:27.160
But when that lava starts to ooze, they'll be running the heels off their shoes.

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:29.800
Heck, we'd have thought she was over-publicized.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:34.040
But the effects of Johnston's words were lost on most people,

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:37.900
who were more interested in Mount St. Helens as just another roadside attraction.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:41.420
It's gonna rock, it's gonna roll, it's gonna leave a nasty hole,

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:44.480
it'll be too late to find a place to hide.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.500
As we know, it's just a joke.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:52.580
Well, not really a joke, I guess, but it could last a hundred years.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:55.500
We're gonna stay 'til the rocks start falling, I guess.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.800
Hey, you don't mind working in the shadow of the mountain, then?

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:58.960
No, not at all.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:07.580
to be scared of. More danger than one of them trees falling on the backside than this mountain

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:15.280
exploding. For 83-year-old Harry Truman, operator of St. Helens Lodge, nothing was about to

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:20.480
move him from the shadow of his beloved mountain. Harry wants to stay, and he's not going to

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:26.400
leave, and he's thanked us for our concern for coming. It's definitely a mistake. Somebody

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:30.000
done it to cause trouble for me and the law and everybody else.

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:35.800
That's my life. Spirit Lake and Mount St. Helens is my life, folks. I've lived there 50 years. It's a part of me.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:39.200
That mountain and that lake is a part of Truman, and I'm a part of it.

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:50.100
The pressure was building, but Harry wouldn't budge.

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:20.070
The morning of May 18, 1980, it was like any other Sunday morning for a Forest Service tree planting crew on the lower southern flank of the mountain, until 8:32 a.m.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:00.000
to a habit.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:08.530
instant a cubic mile tons of ash rock and ice were rocketed into the

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:14.270
stratosphere the cloud reached nearly 14 miles into the sky the view was

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:18.570
spectacular but the wind was blowing in a north easterly direction and from that

00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:23.780
point of view the clouds seemed like the end of the world the skies grew dark and

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:30.000
at noon it was pitch black in most of eastern Washington to not panic if you want to

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.980
start getting out some water supplies now. Life in the ash storm came to a gritty halt.

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:37.720
I assume the road is closed from here or any further east.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:40.060
Okay. This is as far east as you can go.

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:45.980
Those who could move, however, moved quickly. The 304th Aerospace Rescue and Recovery Squadron

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:51.060
from the Portland Air Base was called in to find geologist David Johnston, who was manning

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:56.470
a scientific lookout above Coldwater Creek, six miles northwest of the mountain. He was

00:10:56.470 --> 00:10:57.470
never found.

00:10:57.470 --> 00:10:59.920
This is not the, uh, and nothing matches the map.

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:09.460
Where's Spirit Lake? Is that it over there? I can't believe I've camped up in this area.

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:11.700
Doesn't look like any place I've ever been before.

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:19.190
As most of you already know, we had a major eruption occurring at 8.32 approximately this morning on Mount St. Helens.

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:25.300
The first report that we actually got about the eruption was from Forest Service personnel that were administering a tree planting contract,

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:30.000
and they reported over the radio to us that an eruption was occurring, and it seemed to be about $100.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.910
times greater than one that we've ever had in the past.

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:37.950
It does appear that the northwest flank of the mountain seems to be gone.

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:41.570
They have indicated that there have been some mudflows have come down the southeast flank

00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:42.570
of the mountain.

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:44.810
Emergency procedures have been put into effect.

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:49.580
At 10:15 this morning, the Cowlitz County reported that a 12-foot wall of water was

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:54.270
coming down the Toodle River, and this was verified by the Washington State Patrol.

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:59.090
The largest rescue mission in the history of the northwest was soon underway.

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:01.780
What do they have with that?

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:08.430
Military helicopter units and other rescue squads joined the effort,

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:10.870
coordinated by the county sheriff's departments.

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:14.330
By the end of the rescue operations, which lasted two weeks,

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:17.370
more than 170 people were brought to safety.

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:21.400
But in the final count, 61 were dead or missing.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:26.750
Rotor wash from the helicopters kicked up great clouds of ash,

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:27.830
making rescue difficult.

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:30.000
In many places, it was impossible to land.

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:36.070
rescuers were lowered by cable to assist survivors who were then reeled to safety.

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:42.670
Tons of ice and snow melted by the intense heat of the raging volcano

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:47.480
mixed with the ash and began rampaging down the flanks of the volcano.

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:51.910
One mudflow reached Swift Reservoir, about 10 miles to the south,

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:58.020
but it was the debris-laden walls of water which tore down the north fork of the Toodle River

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:00.000
that proved most spectacular and devastating.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:24.560
stadium after wiping out an entire logging camp the deluge raged on in a matter of hours this river

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:30.000
of destruction surged under the tootle river bridge on interstate 5 closing the main arterial between

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:36.630
Portland and Seattle. Entire forests and logs, hundreds of years old, roared past stunned on

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:42.430
those. From the Tootle to the Cowlitz to the Columbia River, first attempts at clearing the

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:48.690
debris became exercises in futility. For the first time in decades, the lower Columbia River

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:57.890
was closed to freighter traffic. The raging torrent had left its mark.

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:18.050
In a matter of hours, the winds carried the growing ash plume over Idaho and Montana.

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:22.770
A month later, the plume had circled the entire earth.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:30.000
In the wake of the plume, ash was everywhere.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:44.970
everywhere. In some places the price of a surgical mask quadrupled. Ash doesn't melt.

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:52.090
Water turns it into a thick, grimy substance like wet cement. It's as back-breaking a job

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:53.780
as moving a mountain should be.

00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:12.460
When the skies finally cleared, Gifford Pinchot National Forest personnel began the sad task of assessing the damage.

00:15:12.460 --> 00:15:26.400
Acres upon acres of prime timber flattened, bridges, roads, campgrounds, trails, all obliterated, buried, destroyed.

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:37.680
Spirit Lake was a steaming quagmire of debris. The entire north flank of the mountain was gone,

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:44.010
and that proved the key to understanding exactly what had occurred on the morning of May 18th.

00:15:44.010 --> 00:15:51.480
The initial event was a major earthquake. A devastating lateral explosion followed, destroying

00:15:51.480 --> 00:16:00.000
nearly every living thing in its path, including Harry Truman. The initial force of the eruption had been across

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:06.110
the land instead of blasting skyward. Now the mountain, which lost most of its

00:16:06.110 --> 00:16:11.690
summit, is topped by a gaping horseshoe-shaped crater nearly two miles long

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:23.880
and one mile wide. The perilous vantage point of a still photographer and the

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:30.000
actual recorded voice of an eyewitness with a two-way radio bring the tremendous force of the May 18th

00:16:30.000 --> 00:17:00.000
eruption into chilling focus.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:08.800
the whole northwest section and north section blowing up, crying dead. It's coming up over the ridge towards me. I'm going to back out of here.

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:16.890
Photographer Gary Rosenquist barely escaped. Radio operator Jerry Martin did not.

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:24.070
A recovery and planning team undertook an immediate survey of the area and began one of the most

00:17:24.070 --> 00:17:30.000
intensive planning efforts in the history of the Forest Service. Their preferred plan called for extended

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.790
protection of this window into the Earth's building process while recovering a

00:17:34.790 --> 00:17:43.270
portion of the downed timber and rehabilitating the land. Although at much

00:17:43.270 --> 00:17:53.790
lesser intensity, the mountain erupted five more times during 1980. This eruption

00:17:53.790 --> 00:18:00.000
on July 22nd was photographed on time-lapse by amateur photographer Jerry Whittaker of

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:11.730
Woodland, Washington. Meanwhile, a watchful eye is kept on the steamy crater and surrounding area.

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:20.470
Some say the mountain shows signs of going back to sleep. Others say that's not likely yet. But

00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:30.000
the fact is, no one knows for sure. And so the vigil continues. Geological surveys

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:53.280
scientists visit the crater and surrounding area as frequently as conditions permit samples are

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:59.670
collected distances between established points are measured electronically to detect any swelling

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.130
deflation of the volcano.

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:14.440
Forest Service and University scientists continue to monitor how plant and animal life are recovering.

00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:44.250
Volcanoes are secretive and unpredictable things.

00:19:44.870 --> 00:19:49.660
St. Helens could stay active for a week, a decade, longer.

00:19:50.450 --> 00:19:56.030
But sooner or later, by one manner or another, she will change again.

00:19:57.250 --> 00:19:58.870
That's the way it is with volcanoes.

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:03.050
To be sure, much will happen in the meantime.

00:20:03.050 --> 00:20:08.760
Trees and huckleberries and wild animals will return to the mountain's flanks.

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:13.290
Spirit Lake will become clear and blue-green again.

00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:18.980
Gurgling streams will again be favorite haunts of the trout and the fishermen.

00:20:18.980 --> 00:20:23.260
But for now, we have a landscape in transition.

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:29.670
A rare opportunity to learn from the earth what the earth is made of, why it changes.

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:32.360
and perhaps even when it will change again.

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:39.760
For those who find the loss of a mountain as it once was difficult to bear,

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:43.550
there is consolation in a long-range program

00:20:43.750 --> 00:20:47.890
which will protect the marvel of the area's natural recovery,

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:53.650
allowing many generations to replay the instant of destruction

00:20:54.140 --> 00:21:00.000
while at the same time being eyewitnesses to the magnificent healing powers of nature.
